use std
into pack.amni

This file is a module with no main (namespace pack). It unpacks gzip and bzip2 data held in memory and reads tar archives held in memory (several can be open at once, each named by an integer handle, like the zip module). Every module-level name starts with `pk_` or `PK_`. Problems are reported with `dm::dm_warn_add`, and a function that fails leaves its output list empty.

```amni
import dm;
import gfx.zlib;
```

`var pk_cap = 268435456` is the most bytes one decompression may produce (256 MiB); the tests lower it. `var pk_capped = false` is set when that limit was hit. `pk_over() bool` sets `pk_capped` to true, adds the warning "decompressed data passes the limit of 256 MiB" with `dm::dm_warn_add` and returns false.

`pk_le(s const byte[:], p int, n int) int` reads an unsigned little-endian number of n bytes (1, 2 or 4) at offset p of s, or -1 when p is negative or p plus n passes the end of s.

`var pk_crc_t int[>..]` and `var pk_crc_b int[>..]` are two tables of 256 ints, built once by `pk_crc_init()` when `pk_crc_t` is empty. Entry n of `pk_crc_t` (the reflected CRC-32 of gzip) starts as n and eight times becomes (c shifted right by one) xor 0xEDB88320 when its lowest bit is set, and c shifted right by one when not. Entry n of `pk_crc_b` (the MSB-first CRC-32 of bzip2) starts as n shifted left by 24 and eight times becomes ((c shifted left by one) xor 0x04C11DB7) and 0xFFFFFFFF when bit 31 of c is set, and (c shifted left by one) and 0xFFFFFFFF when not.

`pk_crc32(s const byte[:], crc int) int` continues a gzip CRC-32 over s: it calls `pk_crc_init()`, starts with c equal to crc xor 0xFFFFFFFF, for every byte b sets c to `pk_crc_t` at index (c xor b) and 255, xor (c shifted right by 8), and returns c xor 0xFFFFFFFF. Call it with crc 0 to start.

`pk_bzcrc(s const byte[:], crc int) int` continues a bzip2 CRC over s: it calls `pk_crc_init()`, starts with c equal to crc, for every byte b sets c to ((c shifted left by 8) and 0xFFFFFFFF) xor `pk_crc_b` at index ((c shifted right by 24) xor b) and 255, and returns c (the running register; the caller starts at 0xFFFFFFFF and xors the final register with 0xFFFFFFFF).

Inflate. The deflate bit reader lives in module variables: `var pk_pos = 0` (the next byte of the input to load), `var pk_acc = 0` (bits not yet used, the lowest bit is next), `var pk_have = 0` (how many bits `pk_acc` holds) and `var pk_bad = false`.

`pk_ifill(src const byte[:])` loads bytes while `pk_have` is at most 48: when `pk_pos` is below the length of src, add the byte at `pk_pos` shifted left by `pk_have` bits to `pk_acc` and add 1 to `pk_pos`; otherwise when `pk_pos` is below the length of src plus 8 add nothing but still add 1 to `pk_pos` (a virtual zero byte); otherwise set `pk_bad` to true and stop the loop. Every byte taken, real or virtual, adds 8 to `pk_have`.

`pk_take(src const byte[:], n int) int` returns 0 when n is 0 or `pk_bad` is set; otherwise, when `pk_have` is below n it calls `pk_ifill(src)` first and returns 0 when `pk_bad` is now set; then it returns the lowest n bits of `pk_acc`, shifts `pk_acc` right by n and subtracts n from `pk_have`.

`pk_isym(src const byte[:], c zlib::Code&) int` decodes one Huffman symbol with the tables c exactly like the inner `symbol` function of `zlib::inflate_raw`: when `pk_have` is below 16 call `pk_ifill(src)`; let e be `c.fast` at index `pk_acc` and 511; when e is not 0, the code length is e and 15: shift `pk_acc` right by that, subtract it from `pk_have` and return e shifted right by 4. Otherwise decode one bit at a time: code, first and index start at 0; for l from 1 to 15: code becomes code or `pk_take(src, 1)`, n is `c.count` at l, when code minus first is below n return `c.symbol` at index plus code minus first, then index grows by n, first becomes (first plus n) shifted left by one and code is shifted left by one. When no length matched, set `pk_bad` and return -1.

`pk_inflate(src const byte[:], start int, out byte[>..]&, endp int&) bool` decodes one raw DEFLATE stream (RFC 1951) that begins at byte start of src and appends the result to out, which may already hold earlier data (a match may only reach back to where this stream's own output began, so remember out's length at the start as the base). It sets `pk_pos` to start and `pk_acc`, `pk_have` to 0 and `pk_bad` to false, then repeats until a block with the final flag is done: last is `pk_take(src, 1)`, kind is `pk_take(src, 2)`. Kind 0 (stored): drop `pk_have` modulo 8 bits, read the length (16 bits) and its complement (16 bits) and fail when the complement is not the length xor 0xFFFF, fail through `pk_over()` when out would grow past `pk_cap`, then copy that many bytes taken with `pk_take(src, 8)`. Kinds 1 and 2 use the tables `lit` and `dist` that are `zlib::empty_code()` values built with `zlib::build(code, lens)` (it takes an i32 slice and returns false for an over-subscribed code) from a scratch array `lens` of 320 i32. Kind 1 (fixed): lengths 8 for symbols 0 to 143, 9 for 144 to 255, 7 for 256 to 279 and 8 for 280 to 287 build `lit` (from 288 entries) and 5 for 30 entries builds `dist`. Kind 2 (dynamic): nlit is `pk_take(src, 5)` plus 257, ndist is `pk_take(src, 5)` plus 1, ncl is `pk_take(src, 4)` plus 4; read ncl lengths of 3 bits each into an array of 19 i32 zeros at the positions `zlib::CL_ORDER` gives, build the code-length code `clc` from it, then fill `lens` for nlit plus ndist entries: a symbol below 16 is a length; 16 repeats the previous length 3 plus `pk_take(src, 2)` times (fail at index 0); 17 repeats 0 for 3 plus `pk_take(src, 3)` times; 18 repeats 0 for 11 plus `pk_take(src, 7)` times; fail when a repeat passes nlit plus ndist or a symbol is negative; fail when nlit is above 286 or ndist above 30; build `lit` from the first nlit lengths and `dist` from a 32-entry copy of the following ndist lengths (`dist` lengths copied into a fresh zeroed i32 array of 32 and sliced to ndist). Then loop: s is `pk_isym(src, lit)`; fail when `pk_bad` is set or s is negative; below 256 push the byte (failing through `pk_over()` first when out already holds `pk_cap` bytes); 256 ends the block; otherwise li is s minus 257 (fail when 29 or more), the length is `zlib::LEN_BASE` at li plus `pk_take(src, zlib::LEN_EXTRA at li)`, ds is `pk_isym(src, dist)` (fail when negative or 30 or more), the distance is `zlib::DIST_BASE` at ds plus `pk_take(src, zlib::DIST_EXTRA at ds)`; fail when the distance is more than out's length minus the base; fail through `pk_over()` when out plus the length would pass `pk_cap`; then push that many bytes one at a time, each copied from the distance back (the copy may overlap). Kind 3 fails. After every block fail when `pk_bad` is set. At the end set endp to `pk_pos` minus `pk_have` divided by 8 (whole unused bytes handed back) and return false when endp is more than the length of src (the data ran out), otherwise true.

`var pk_gzn byte[>..]` holds the stored file name of the last gunzipped data. `pk_gz_name() const byte[:]` returns it (empty when there was none).

`pk_is_gz(d const byte[:]) bool` is true when d has at least 18 bytes and starts with the bytes 0x1F, 0x8B, 8. `pk_is_bz2(d const byte[:]) bool` is true when d has at least 14 bytes, starts with "BZh", has a digit from 1 to 9 as its fourth byte, and bytes 4 to 9 are either 0x31 0x41 0x59 0x26 0x53 0x59 or 0x17 0x72 0x45 0x38 0x50 0x90.

`pk_gunzip(data const byte[:], out byte[>..]&) bool` clears out and `pk_gzn`, sets `pk_capped` to false and decompresses gzip data (RFC 1952) made of one or more members one after another. Fail (clearing out and adding one warning, "gzip: not a gzip file" when the data does not satisfy `pk_is_gz`; for every other failure "gzip: corrupt or truncated data" unless `pk_capped` is set) when anything below goes wrong. For each member at offset p: the header is the bytes 0x1F, 0x8B, 8 (the method), the flags byte, 4 bytes of time, 1 of extra flags and 1 of operating system, 10 bytes in all; fail when any of the flag bits 5 to 7 (value 0xE0) are set. Bit 2 (value 4) means an extra field follows: its 2-byte little-endian length and that many bytes are skipped. Bit 3 (value 8) means a zero-terminated original file name follows: for the first member only, store its bytes (up to the zero) in `pk_gzn` converted with `dm::dm_to_utf8`; fail when there is no terminating zero before the end of the data. Bit 4 (value 16) means a zero-terminated comment follows, skipped the same way. Bit 1 (value 2) means a 2-byte header checksum follows that must equal the lowest 16 bits of `pk_crc32` of the header bytes before it (from the member's start); fail when it does not. Then call `pk_inflate` at that offset with the member's own start length of out remembered, giving endp; the trailer is 8 bytes at endp: the CRC-32 of this member's output and its length modulo 2 to the power 32, both little-endian (read with `pk_le`); fail when the data ends before them, when `pk_crc32` of this member's output (out from the remembered start) differs (warning "gzip: CRC mismatch" instead of the general one) or when the length differs (warning "gzip: length mismatch"). The next member starts at endp plus 8. When bytes remain: when they start with 0x1F 0x8B treat them as another member; otherwise, unless every remaining byte is 0, add the warning "gzip: trailing bytes ignored" and stop. Return true after at least one member decoded.

Bzip2 reads bits most significant first. Its bit reader is `var pk_bacc = 0` (the unused bits, exactly `pk_bhave` of them, in its low part), `var pk_bhave = 0`, `var pk_bpos = 0` (the next input byte) and `var pk_bbad = false`. `pk_bbits(src const byte[:], n int) int` (n at most 24) returns 0 when `pk_bbad` is set; otherwise while `pk_bhave` is below n and at most 48 it appends the byte at `pk_bpos` to the low end of `pk_bacc` (shift left by 8, or in the byte), adds 1 to `pk_bpos` and 8 to `pk_bhave`, and when `pk_bpos` is already at the end of src it sets `pk_bbad` and returns 0 instead; then it returns the top n of the `pk_bhave` bits, lowers `pk_bhave` by n and clears the bits of `pk_bacc` that are no longer counted (and with 1 shifted left by `pk_bhave` minus 1).

Bzip2 module state: `var pk_tt int[>..]` (the block being decoded; the low 8 bits of an entry are a byte, the rest a link), `var pk_unz int[>..]` (256 byte counts), `var pk_mtf int[>..]` (256 entries), `var pk_bmap int[>..]` (the bytes in use), `var pk_bsel int[>..]` (selectors), `var pk_hlen int[>..]` (code lengths of one table), `var pk_hcnt int[>..]` (counts of codes per length: 6 tables of 21, table t length l at t times 21 plus l) and `var pk_hsym int[>..]` (symbols sorted by code: 6 tables of 258, table t position k at t times 258 plus k). Make `pk_hcnt` 126 long and `pk_hsym` 1548 long (pushed zeros) the first time they are used.

`pk_bzmap(src const byte[:]) int` reads the symbol map: 16 bits "used" (`pk_bbits`), then for each of the 16 groups i from the top bit of used, when that bit is set, 16 more bits whose set bits (from the top) j mean byte i times 16 plus j is in use. It clears `pk_bmap`, pushes every byte in use in increasing order and returns how many (0 for none or when `pk_bbad` is set).

`pk_bzsel(src const byte[:], ngroups int) int` reads the selectors: the count is `pk_bbits(src, 15)`; return -1 when it is 0 or `pk_bbad` is set. Each selector is coded in unary: count one bits until a zero bit (fail with -1 when the count reaches ngroups), that number is a position in a move-to-front list that starts as 0, 1, 2, and so on up to ngroups minus 1; the selector is the list entry at that position, which then moves to the front of the list. Clear `pk_bsel`, push the selectors in order and return their count.

`pk_bztab(src const byte[:], t int, asize int) bool` reads the code lengths of table t (asize symbols) and builds its decoding tables: first `pk_bbits(src, 5)` is the starting length; for each symbol: repeat { fail unless the length is from 1 to 20; read one bit; when it is 0 stop; read another bit: 0 adds 1 to the length and 1 subtracts 1 }, then store the length in `pk_hlen` (cleared first, one entry per symbol). Then zero the 21 counts of table t in `pk_hcnt`, count the lengths into them, fail when the code over-subscribes (left starts at 1 and for each length from 1 to 20 becomes left times 2 minus count; fail when negative), and fill `pk_hsym` for table t with the symbol numbers ordered by length (shortest first) and, within a length, by symbol number. Return false when `pk_bbad` is set.

`pk_bzdec(src const byte[:], t int) int` decodes one symbol with table t the canonical way (like the `decode` function of zlib's puff.c, reading bits most significant first with `pk_bbits(src, 1)`): code, first and index start at 0; for length l from 1 to 20: code becomes code or the next bit, count is `pk_hcnt` at t times 21 plus l; when code minus count is below first return `pk_hsym` at t times 258 plus index plus (code minus first); otherwise index grows by count, first grows by count and is then shifted left by one, and code is shifted left by one. Return -1 when nothing matched or `pk_bbad` is set.

`pk_bzdata(src const byte[:], ngroups int, nsel int, nin int, bsize int) int` decodes the block's symbols with the tables and the selectors, filling `pk_tt` (first make sure it holds at least bsize entries by pushing zeros) and `pk_unz` (256 zeros first). The symbols are 0 (RUNA), 1 (RUNB), 2 up to nin (a move-to-front index, the symbol minus 1) and nin plus 1 (end of block). `pk_mtf` starts as 0, 1, 2 and so on for nin entries. Every 50 symbols the next selector chooses the table (fail with -1 when the selectors run out). A run of RUNA and RUNB symbols is a number in bijective base 2: the run starts at 0 with a weight of 1; RUNA adds the weight, RUNB adds twice the weight, and the weight doubles after each (fail when the weight reaches 2097152). When a run of them ends (any other symbol arrives) the run length is that number: the byte `pk_bmap` at `pk_mtf` entry 0 is stored that many times in `pk_tt` (fail when that passes bsize) and added to its count in `pk_unz`. A move-to-front symbol takes the entry at its position from `pk_mtf`, moves it to the front, and stores the byte `pk_bmap` of it in `pk_tt` (failing when the block already holds bsize entries) and counts it in `pk_unz`. At the end of block symbol finish any pending run first. Return the number of bytes in the block, or -1 on any failure or when `pk_bbad` is set.

`pk_bzout(nblock int, orig int, out byte[>..]&) bool` runs the inverse Burrows-Wheeler transform on `pk_tt` and appends the decoded bytes of the block to out, undoing the initial run-length coding on the way. First build the starting positions: cumulative sums of `pk_unz` (position of byte b is the sum of the counts of the bytes below it) in a local int array of 256; for i from 0 to nblock minus 1: u is `pk_tt` at i and 255, `pk_tt` at the start position of u gets i shifted left by 8 or-ed in, and that start position grows by 1. Then tpos is `pk_tt` at orig shifted right by 8; repeat nblock times: tpos becomes `pk_tt` at tpos; the byte is tpos and 255; tpos is shifted right by 8. Each byte goes through the run-length decoder: run starts at 0 and last at -1; when run is 4 the byte is a repeat count: append last that many more times and set run to 0 and last to -1; otherwise when the byte equals last add 1 to run, else set last to the byte and run to 1, and append the byte. Fail through `pk_over()` when out would pass `pk_cap`. Return true.

`pk_bzblock(src const byte[:], out byte[>..]&, bsize int, crc int&) bool` decodes one block whose 48-bit magic was already read: stored is the 32-bit block CRC (two reads of 16 bits, high half first); the next bit is the randomised flag (fail when set, warning "bzip2: randomised blocks are not supported"); orig is 24 bits; nin is `pk_bzmap(src)` (fail when 0); ngroups is `pk_bbits(src, 3)` (fail unless 2 to 6); nsel is `pk_bzsel(src, ngroups)` (fail when negative); `pk_bztab` for every table 0 to ngroups minus 1 with alphabet size nin plus 2; nblock is `pk_bzdata(src, ngroups, nsel, nin, bsize)` (fail when it is 0 or negative, or when orig is not below it). Remember out's length, call `pk_bzout`, and compare: the block CRC is `pk_bzcrc` of what the block appended, starting at 0xFFFFFFFF, xor 0xFFFFFFFF; fail with the warning "bzip2: CRC mismatch" when it differs from stored. Set crc to ((crc shifted left by one) or (crc shifted right by 31)) and 0xFFFFFFFF, xor the block CRC. Return true.

`pk_bzstream(src const byte[:], p int, out byte[>..]&, endp int&) bool` decodes one bzip2 stream that starts at byte p: set `pk_bpos` to p, `pk_bhave`, `pk_bacc` to 0 and `pk_bbad` to false; read the bytes "B", "Z", "h" and a digit 1 to 9 with `pk_bbits(src, 8)` (fail otherwise), bsize is that digit times 100000; combined starts at 0. Repeat: read a 48-bit magic as two 24-bit reads; when it is 0x314159 then 0x265359 decode a block with `pk_bzblock` and update the combined CRC through it; when it is 0x177245 then 0x385090 read the 32-bit stored CRC (two 16-bit reads), fail when it differs from combined (with the warning "bzip2: CRC mismatch"), set endp to `pk_bpos` minus `pk_bhave` divided by 8 (the rest of the last byte is padding) and return true; any other magic fails; fail whenever `pk_bbad` is set.

`pk_bunzip(data const byte[:], out byte[>..]&) bool` clears out, sets `pk_capped` to false and decompresses bzip2 data made of one or more streams one after another: it needs `pk_is_bz2` for the first stream (otherwise the warning "bzip2: not a bzip2 stream"); each stream is decoded with `pk_bzstream`; when at least 4 bytes remain and they start with "BZh" and a digit from 1 to 9 they are another stream (a cut or damaged one makes the whole call fail), otherwise unless every remaining byte is 0 add the warning "bzip2: trailing bytes ignored" and stop. When a stream fails, clear out, add the warning "bzip2: corrupt or truncated data" (unless `pk_capped` is set or a more specific warning was just given by the block) and return false. Return true after at least one stream.

Tar. `struct PkTar` has `base int` (where its bytes start in `pk_tdata`), `len int`, `first int` (the index of its first entry in `pk_tents`), `nbase int` (where its names start in `pk_tnames`), `cnt int` and `live bool`. `struct PkTEnt` has `nat int` and `nlen int` (the name bytes in `pk_tnames`), `size int`, `doff int` (the offset of the data from the start of its archive) and `kind int` (the type flag as a character code: 0x30 for a regular file, after turning a zero byte and 0x37 into 0x30; 0x35 a directory; 0x31 hard link, 0x32 symbolic link, 0x33, 0x34 and 0x36 devices and fifo; any other byte as it is). Module state: `var pk_tdata byte[>..]`, `var pk_tnames byte[>..]`, `var pk_tars PkTar[>..]` and `var pk_tents PkTEnt[>..]`. A handle is an index into `pk_tars`.

`pk_oct(s const byte[:]) int` reads a tar number field: when the first byte is 128 or more it is a big-endian binary number in the remaining bytes (the first byte and 127 is its top part), returning -1 when the second bit (64) of the first byte is set or when the value passes 2 to the power 48 (check after every byte before shifting, so nothing can overflow); otherwise skip leading spaces and zero bytes, read octal digits up to a space or zero byte or the end, and return -1 when any other byte is met. An empty field is 0.

`pk_zero(d const byte[:], p int) bool` is true when the 512 bytes at p of d are all zero. `pk_hdr(d const byte[:], p int) bool` is true when 512 bytes at p lie inside d, they are not all zero, the checksum field (8 bytes at p plus 148) reads with `pk_oct` as a number that is 0 or more and equals the sum of the 512 bytes with the 8 checksum bytes counted as spaces (32 each), or the same sum taken with signed bytes (128 to 255 counted as minus 256 less), and the size field (12 bytes at p plus 124) reads with `pk_oct` as 0 or more.

`pk_cstr(d const byte[:], from int, n int) const byte[:]` is the slice of d from from up to the first zero byte or n bytes, whichever comes first.

`pk_pax(rec const byte[:], path byte[>..]&, havepath bool&, size int&)` reads a pax extended header: records are "LEN key=value" and a newline, LEN being the decimal length of the whole record including itself and the newline; stop at the first record whose LEN is less than 3, not a number or passes the end. A record "path" clears path, appends its value and sets havepath; a record "size" sets size to its value read as decimal (ignored when it is not a number or has more than 12 digits). Other keys are ignored.

`pk_tar_open(data const byte[:]) int` returns a handle of 0 or more, or -1 when data is not tar (fewer than 512 bytes, or the first block fails `pk_hdr`, so an empty or all-zero archive is -1 too). Remember `pk_tnames.len`; walk the blocks with p starting at 0 and a list of entries, keeping `lname` (a long name from a GNU type L entry, with its flag), global path and size overrides from type g entries (size -1 when none) and the pending per-entry overrides from type x entries. While 512 bytes remain at p: stop at an all-zero block (`pk_zero`); stop when `pk_hdr` fails there (when this is the first block return -1; later it means the data is damaged: add the warning "tar: damaged header, listing stops" and stop); name is `pk_cstr` of the first 100 bytes; when the 5 bytes at p plus 257 are "ustar" and the 155-byte prefix field at p plus 345 is not empty the name is the prefix, a slash and the name; size is `pk_oct` of the field; kind is the byte at p plus 156; the data starts at doff equal to p plus 512. A kind of 0x78 (x), 0x67 (g), 0x4C (L) or 0x4B (K) is a meta entry whose data is the next size bytes (stop the walk when they pass the end of data): x gives per-entry overrides via `pk_pax` (path and size), g gives the global ones likewise, L gives the long name (its bytes up to the first zero byte), K is skipped; then p moves on to doff plus size rounded up to a multiple of 512, and the walk continues without adding an entry. Any other kind is an entry: its name is the x path when set, else the L name when set, else the global path when set, else the header name; its size is the x size when set (not negative), else the global size when set, else the header size; the per-entry overrides are then cleared. The data of kinds 0x31 to 0x36 is not stored in the archive (the next header follows right after this one), every other kind has its size bytes after the header. Push the name into `pk_tnames` and an entry (kind mapped as in the struct description). Move p past the data rounded up to a multiple of 512. Stop after 4000000 entries. When the walk found no entry return -1 and leave no state changed. Otherwise append data to `pk_tdata` after recording the base, push a `PkTar` with live true and cnt equal to the entry count, and return its index.

`pk_tar_count(h int) int` is the entry count of handle h, or 0 for a bad or closed handle. `pk_tar_name(h int, i int) const byte[:]` is the name of entry i of handle h (a slice of `pk_tnames`), empty for a bad handle or index. `pk_tar_size(h int, i int) int` is the size of entry i, -1 for a bad handle or index. `pk_tar_is_dir(h int, i int) bool` is true when the kind is 0x35 or the name ends in "/". `pk_tar_readable(h int, i int) bool` is true for a regular file (kind 0x30) that is not a directory by name.

`pk_norm_eq(a const byte[:], b const byte[:], ci bool) bool` compares two tar names after normalising each: a backslash counts as a slash, any number of leading "/" or "./" prefixes and trailing "/" are ignored; when ci is true ASCII letters compare without regard to case.

`pk_tar_find(h int, name const byte[:]) int` returns the index of the first entry of handle h whose name equals name byte for byte; failing that the first one for which `pk_norm_eq(entry name, name, false)` holds; failing that the first one for which `pk_norm_eq(entry name, name, true)` holds; -1 when none or the handle is bad.

`pk_tar_read(h int, i int, out byte[>..]&) bool` clears out and appends the contents of entry i of handle h. It returns false (leaving out empty) for a bad handle or index, when the entry is not readable (`pk_tar_readable`), when its size is more than `pk_cap`, or when its data would pass the end of the archive.

`pk_tar_close(h int)` marks handle h not live; then, while the last `PkTar` in `pk_tars` is not live, truncate `pk_tdata` to its base, `pk_tents` to its first, `pk_tnames` to its nbase and pop it. A bad or already closed handle does nothing.
